#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# gaussian_matrix

#% text
# `gaussian_matrix(r)` gives the `2 r + 1` by `2 r + 1` kernel of the Gaussian of standard deviation `r/2`, sampled at the integers and normalized to total 1.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `gaussian_matrix([r, s])` uses the standard deviation `s`.

#% item
# `gaussian_matrix([[r]])` gives the one-dimensional kernel (a vector).

#% item
# The kernel is the outer product of the normalized one-dimensional kernel with itself.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
gaussian_matrix(1)
#% output
#= [[0.011343736558495075, 0.0838195058022106, 0.011343736558495075], [0.0838195058022106, 0.6193470305571773, 0.0838195058022106], [0.011343736558495075, 0.0838195058022106, 0.011343736558495075]]

#% cell
total(flatten(gaussian_matrix(2)))
#% output
#= 1.0

#% cell
gaussian_matrix([2, 1])
#% output
#= [[0.0029690167439504977, 0.013306209891013653, 0.021938231279714643, 0.013306209891013653, 0.0029690167439504977], [0.013306209891013653, 0.05963429543618014, 0.09832033134884577, 0.05963429543618014, 0.013306209891013653], [0.021938231279714643, 0.09832033134884577, 0.16210282163712664, 0.09832033134884577, 0.021938231279714643], [0.013306209891013653, 0.05963429543618014, 0.09832033134884577, 0.05963429543618014, 0.013306209891013653], [0.0029690167439504977, 0.013306209891013653, 0.021938231279714643, 0.013306209891013653, 0.0029690167439504977]]

#% cell
gaussian_matrix([[3]])
#% output
#= [0.03663284536919403, 0.11128075847888486, 0.21674532140370778, 0.27068214949642655, 0.21674532140370778, 0.11128075847888486, 0.03663284536919403]

